{"type":"message","id":"5e2d993f","parentId":"b6869371","timestamp":"2026-03-29T00:12:36.389Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So the user is saying the audio they heard was in English, not Chinese. This means the TTS tool is generating English audio even though I'm passing Chinese text. This could be a TTS tool configuration issue - maybe it defaults to English or detects language incorrectly.\n\nLet me check if there's a way to specify the language for the TTS tool. Looking at the TTS tool description, it just says \"Convert text to speech\" with a \"text\" parameter and an optional \"channel\". There's no language parameter.\n\nBut wait - the TTS might be auto-detecting the language or defaulting to English. Let me try to see if there's a way to influence this. Maybe I need to explicitly set the text in a way that forces Chinese, or check the TTS configuration.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the tts tool result - it delivered the audio automatically as a file. But the user said it was English. That's odd because I sent Chinese text. Maybe the TTS engine (the ElevenLabs model or whatever is configured) is not correctly handling the Chinese text and falling back to English.\n\nLet me try a different approach - maybe I should check if there's a way to set the language for TTS, or maybe the voice/model doesn't support Chinese well and it's defaulting to English.\n\nActually, I should just tell the user what happened and see if they want to try a different approach. The TTS seems to be auto-detecting or defaulting to English despite the Chinese input. This is a technical limitation I can't easily fix without changing the TTS configuration.\n\nLet me also note that the message tool has a `asVoice` parameter - maybe that sends text directly as voice in the target channel's language? Let me think... The `asVoice` parameter says \"Convert text to speech\" so it seems like the message tool itself might be doing TTS. But I was